    rdf:value """Keats. Afterwards, someone said to her it must have been pleasant work, as Keats was so charming a poet. “Yes,” she replied, “but I got so used to looking out for colour words that I could pick out every one on a page and never read any of the poetry.”
We talked about America, and Bernhard begged Lovett not to misunderstand him: “I don’t blame America for what it doesn’t do – but for what it does!”
Thursday June 30. 1898. 3 rue de Beaune. Paris
A letter from William James to Bernhard saying that he has just had time to read his “Central """^^xsd:string ;
